Through rotation commands (ABC, ROT XYZ) we can rotate the flat / shorter side,
of a track to face any direction. The shorter radius is normally the vertical.
Since the pull of gravity flattens the melting pool.
We should consider the up (or down) vector, when setting the shape of the cylinders.

nccp_to_mesh.py, exposed a flaw of the ncc_points generation. The machine paths, between the tracks, are currently mapped to a tid, making them part of a track. We need to think of a solution for the tid value, when LASER=OFF. Perhaps we set it to -1, or we just multiply the current tid with -1. Meaning that negative tid's, indicate that the point is part off an inbetween (machine path) track

